.TH BADCOPY 1 "October 2026" "badcopy" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
badcopy \- copy files that reside (partly) on bad blocks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python badcopy.py
(\fB\-R\fR | \fB\-C\fR)
[\fB\-S\fR \fIsource\fR]
\fB\-D\fR \fIdestination\fR
[\fB\-P\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-O\fR]
.br
.B python badcopy.py
\fB\-H\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B badcopy
copies files from damaged media.
Readable parts are copied.
Unreadable parts are retried and, if they still cannot be read, filled with zeros
so that every offset in the copy matches the original and the copy has the same size.
The source is only ever opened read-only; nothing is written to it.
All output, including the log file, goes to the destination.
.PP
Blocks are read 1 MiB at a time.
A block that fails is split in half repeatedly, down to 512-byte sectors,
so that only the truly unreadable sectors are lost.
Afterwards the remaining bad sectors are re-read for the requested number of
retry passes, and any data recovered is patched into the copy.
.PP
A file that still has unreadable parts is saved as
.IR name _bad. ext
instead of
.IR name . ext .
Files without damage keep their name.
.PP
Switches are case-insensitive (\fB\-r\fR is the same as \fB\-R\fR) and each has a
GNU-style long form.
Option values must be given as a separate argument.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Recurse into subfolders of the source.
The folder tree is preserved below the destination.
Exactly one of \fB\-R\fR and \fB\-C\fR is required.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-current
Copy only the files in the source folder (no subfolders).
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-source " \fIsource\fR"
A file, a folder or a wildcard pattern such as
.IR D:\photos\*.jpg .
Wildcards (\fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB[...]\fR) are allowed only in the file-name part.
If omitted, all files (\fB*.*\fR) in the current folder are used.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-dest ", " \-\-destination " \fIfolder\fR"
Destination folder; created if it does not exist. Required.
If it lies inside the source tree it is excluded from a recursive copy.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-passes " \fIn\fR"
Number of retry passes over sectors that could not be read.
Default 3; 0 disables retrying.
Retries often recover flaky sectors; dead sectors stay dead, so values above
about 5 rarely help and each pass is slow on a failing drive.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-overwrite
Overwrite files that already exist at the destination.
By default such files (under either name, plain or with the \fB_bad\fR suffix)
are skipped.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
After the files are tallied, a summary line
.RI ( "Copying N file(s), X MB in total" )
is printed.
While a file is copied, a single progress line shows the file number, percentage
and bytes done of the file size (in bytes, KB, MB or GB), plus the retry pass when
retrying.
The progress line is shown only when standard output is a terminal.
Each file then gets a result line:
.BR ok ,
.BR BAD ,
.BR skip
or
.BR FAILED .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I destination/badcopy.log
Appended to on every run.
Lists each unreadable range as tab-separated
.RB ( BAD ,
source path, offset, length) and files that could not be opened
.RB ( FAILED ).
.TP
.I *.badcopy.part
Temporary name used while a file is being written; renamed when the file is
complete and removed if the run is aborted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All files copied without bad areas.
.TP
.B 1
Some files had unreadable areas or could not be copied.
.TP
.B 2
Fatal error (for example the destination is full or cannot be created).
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl+C).
.SH EXAMPLES
Copy a whole tree, keeping the folder structure:
.PP
.RS
python badcopy.py \-R \-S D:\photos \-D E:\rescue
.RE
.PP
Copy only the JPEGs in one folder, with five retry passes:
.PP
.RS
python badcopy.py \-C \-S "D:\photos\*.jpg" \-D E:\rescue \-P 5
.RE
.SH NOTES
Requires Python 3.8 or newer and only the standard library.
On heavily damaged disks a sector-level imaging tool such as GNU ddrescue is
a better first step than file-level copying.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR ddrescue (1)
